\[\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}&\sigma(0,1)\arrow[dl,"j_0"']&\\\sigma(0,0)&&\sigma(1,1)\arrow[ul,"i_1"']\arrow[dl,"j_1"]\\&\sigma(1,0)\arrow[ul,"i_0"]&\end{tikzcd}}\]
\unsure{hence} $\begin{cases}i_1\text{ iso}\Longleftrightarrow i_0\text{ iso},\\j_1\text{ iso}\Longleftrightarrow j_0\text{ iso}.\end{cases}$
\margin{Moreover, one must impose this for the induced derivators $\underline D_I$ ($I\in\unsure{\mathrm{Diag}|\mathrm{Dirg}}$), hence in $\underline D(I\times\Delta^1\times\Delta^1)$.}

This last assertion follows from the preceding one (for cart. implies $\Rightarrow$, cocartesian implies $\Leftarrow$), \unsure{let us prove for this} :

``\uline{Lemma}'' \deleted{(associated with Der 6)} : if $\xi=\left(\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}&\xi_a\arrow[dl,"j_0"']\\\xi_0&\\&\xi_b\arrow[ul,"i_0"]\end{tikzcd}}\right)$ is an abstract diagram such that $i_0$ is an iso, then it is isom. to the inverse image of an $\eta\in\underline D(\underline U)$ \deleted{\unsure{\xi}} (where $\underline U=(\unsure{0}\longrightarrow a)$ \inserted{$\simeq\Delta^1$} is the canonical left open of $\underline\Psi$) under the \unsure{retraction} of $\underline\Psi$ onto $\underline U$.

It suffices to prove that
\[\deleted{\unsure{\xi}}\quad\rho^*\rho_*(\xi)-\xi\qquad\text{the adj.}\]
is iso. Since $\rho$ is (\unsure{of course} smooth, as induced by $\mathrm{pr}_1:\Delta^1\times\Delta^1-\Delta^1$ on an open) but also \unsure{proper}, $\rho_*(\xi)$ is computed fibre by fibre. We use that \deleted{\unsure{on}}
\[\mathcal A\simeq\underline D(e)\longrightarrow\underline D^{\mathrm{lc}}(\Delta^1)\]
is an equiv. of cat. (\uline{form of Der 3})$^*$
\margin{\deleted{\unsure{One must argue}\illegible{2}\unsure{here}}}
